"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[46342],{146342:e=>{e.exports="# Per-Page Sub-Agent Prompt\n\nHow to write the prompt body for a single Phase E per-page call to `sub_dashboardagent`. The system prompt owns sequencing, retry, and skip behavior; this skill owns the prompt content for each page.\n\n## When this applies\n\n- The `.pbit` archive contained a `Report/Layout` (the success message from `parseSemanticModel` mentions \"viz migration available\" and `result.layoutFilename` is set).\n- The user confirmed the dashboard-build plan in Phase D.\n- You are dispatching one AI/BI page at a time, sequentially, via the `sub_dashboardagent` tool that the system prompt wires up.\n\nIf `Report/Layout` was absent (model-only `.pbit`) or the user opted out of the dashboard-build branch, skip Phase E's dashboard portion entirely and go straight to the final report.\n\n## Power BI visual → AI/BI widget mapping\n\nPower BI report visuals don't map 1:1 to AI/BI widget types. Use this table when authoring each per-page prompt; the chart-type slot in the template feeds the sub-agent.\n\n| Power BI visual               | AI/BI widget                      | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Card                          | `counter`                         | Single KPI; `height ≤ 2`. Use a `format:` block on the underlying measure for currency / number formatting.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Multi-row card                | multiple `counter` widgets        | One counter per measure; arrange in a row. Don't try to reproduce the multi-row layout as a single widget.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| Clustered / stacked bar       | `bar`                             | The encoded `color:` field reproduces the stacking; let the dashboard agent pick stacked vs. grouped via options.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| Clustered / stacked column    | `bar` (with horizontal layout)    | AI/BI's `bar` covers both orientations.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| Line chart                    | `line`                            | Temporal x-axis; verify the underlying date column is exposed as a dimension.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Line + clustered column combo | `combo`                           | AI/BI's `combo` widget puts **bars on the primary (left) axis** and **lines on the secondary (right) axis** on a shared X axis. To migrate a Power BI line+column visual: put the column-encoded measure(s) into `yAxisPrimary` (bars/left), the line-encoded measure(s) into `yAxisSecondary` (lines/right). Inverting these renders the visual incorrectly. |\n| Area / stacked area           | `area`                            | —                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| Pie / donut                   | `pie`                             | —                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| Scatter / bubble              | `scatter`                         | Bubble size → encoded as a `size:` channel where supported.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| Matrix                        | `pivot`                           | AI/BI's `pivot` widget is the cross-tab / row-and-column-grouped aggregation visualization — use it for Matrix; reserve `table` for flat tabular layouts.                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| Table                         | `table`                           | Flat row-by-row tabular display. AI/BI tables expose a strict schema; consult the metric-view authoring skill before authoring.                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| Funnel                        | `funnel` if available, else `bar` | Many funnel-style visuals reduce to a sorted bar without loss.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Gauge                         | `counter` with format hints       | No native gauge widget; counter + colored format is the closest equivalent.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| KPI                           | `counter`                         | The Power BI KPI's \"trend\" sparkline is dropped; surface it as a `cannot_migrate` note if the user asks.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| Slicer                        | filter widget (page-level)        | See \"Slicer → filter widget\" below.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| Map / filled map / shape map  | varies                            | If an AI/BI map widget exists for the data shape, use it; otherwise mark `unmappable_visual`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| Decomposition tree            | `unmappable_visual`               | No AI/BI equivalent. Skip; surface to the user in the final report.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| AI / Q&A / Smart narrative    | `unmappable_visual`               | AI/BI has its own assistant surface; these PBI widgets don't transfer.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Custom (.pbiviz)              | `unmappable_visual` by default    | Only attempt if a clear AI/BI equivalent exists; otherwise skip.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n\nAnything you mark `unmappable_visual` is recorded with that reason in the final report.\n\n## Slicer → filter widget\n\nPower BI slicers come in three flavors that map to AI/BI filter widgets:\n\n| Power BI slicer type          | AI/BI filter type    | Notes                                                                                              |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Single-select dropdown / tile | `dropdown`           | One value at a time.                                                                               |\n| Multi-select / list           | `multi-select`       | Multiple values OR'd together.                                                                     |\n| Between / range               | `range` or `numeric` | For numeric measures; `between` UI reads cleanly as a range slider.                                |\n| Date / relative date          | `date-range`         | Use the date-range widget; don't try to recreate the relative-date convenience presets one-by-one. |\n\nFor the **slicer-dispatch idiom** (`SWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(slicer[col]), ...)`), the underlying DAX measures should already have been decomposed into per-branch measures during Phase C (see `dax-patterns.md` \xa75 and `architecture-patterns.md` \xa72). In Phase E:\n\n1. Add a `dropdown` filter widget bound to the dimension that drove the original slicer-dispatch (or to a synthetic dimension you created from the dispatch options).\n2. The widget feeds a dashboard parameter or a per-widget filter.\n3. Each affected widget binds to one of the per-branch measures **conditional on the parameter** — typical realization is a `WHERE variant = :param` filter, not a measure rewrite.\n\nThe dashboard authoring sub-agent handles the wiring detail; your job is to specify the slicer's dimension, the affected widgets, and the conditional in the prompt template below.\n\n## Layout — Power BI report grid → AI/BI 12-column grid\n\nPower BI report layout uses an absolute pixel grid (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height` per visual). AI/BI uses a 12-column logical grid. Translation rules:\n\n- Power BI report typical canvas: 1280 px wide. Divide each visual's `x` and `width` by ~107 (1280/12) to get column positions.\n- Round to integer columns and stack rows where the original layout had narrow gutters.\n- Counters (height ≤ 2 in AI/BI): only when the source visual is a single Card or KPI.\n- Filter widgets: typically `height = 1`.\n\nExpress positions in the per-page template's `layout: { row, column, width, height }` slots.\n\n## Per-page prompt template\n\nAdapt this template per page. The sub-agent has no access to the Power BI report, the parsed DataModelSchema, or any prior conversation — every prompt must re-state the context the call needs (datasets, widgets, filters, layout).\n\n```\nCreate dashboard page \"<page_displayName>\" (name: <page_name>; ordering: <0-based index>).\n\nDatasets to register on the dashboard for this page (idempotent — skip if already present):\n- <metric_view_name_1> — <displayName>\n- <metric_view_name_2> — <displayName>\n\nWidgets:\n\n1. <widget_title>\n   chartType: <bar|line|area|pie|scatter|counter|table|pivot|combo|...>\n   dataset: <dataset_name>\n   layout: { row: <r>, column: <c>, width: <w>, height: <h> }   # 12-col grid; counters: height ≤ 2\n   encodings:\n     x: { field: <dimension_name>, scale: <categorical|temporal|quantitative> }\n     y: { field: MEASURE(<measure_name>), scale: quantitative,\n          format: { formatType: currency, currencyCode: USD, decimalPlaces: 2, abbreviation: none } }   # format on the column\n     color: { field: <dimension_name>, scale: categorical }     # optional\n   filters:\n     - <field> <op> <value>                                       # optional, widget-scoped\n   sort:\n     - field: <field>; direction: <asc|desc>                      # optional\n   options:\n     showDataLabels: <true|false>                                 # optional\n     legend: <true|false>                                         # optional\n\n2. <next widget>\n...\n\nFilters on the page (page-level — separate from widget-scoped filters):\n- field: <dimension_name>; widget type: <dropdown|multi-select|date-range|range>; layout: { row, column, width, height }; applies to widgets: [<widget_titles>]\n- ...\n```\n\n## Worked example — counter + bar + line on one page\n\n```\nCreate dashboard page \"Sales Overview\" (name: overview; ordering: 0).\n\nDatasets to register on the dashboard for this page (idempotent — skip if already present):\n- sales_metrics — Sales Metrics\n\nWidgets:\n\n1. Total Revenue\n   chartType: counter\n   dataset: sales_metrics\n   layout: { row: 0, column: 0, width: 4, height: 2 }\n   encodings:\n     value: { field: MEASURE(total_revenue), scale: quantitative,\n              format: { formatType: currency, currencyCode: USD, decimalPlaces: 2, abbreviation: none } }\n\n2. Revenue by Region\n   chartType: bar\n   dataset: sales_metrics\n   layout: { row: 0, column: 4, width: 8, height: 4 }\n   encodings:\n     x: { field: region, scale: categorical }\n     y: { field: MEASURE(total_revenue), scale: quantitative }\n     color: { field: region, scale: categorical }\n   sort:\n     - field: MEASURE(total_revenue); direction: desc\n\n3. Daily Revenue\n   chartType: line\n   dataset: sales_metrics\n   layout: { row: 4, column: 0, width: 12, height: 4 }\n   encodings:\n     x: { field: order_date, scale: temporal }\n     y: { field: MEASURE(total_revenue), scale: quantitative }\n\nFilters on the page:\n- field: region; widget type: multi-select; layout: { row: 8, column: 0, width: 4, height: 1 }; applies to widgets: [Revenue by Region, Daily Revenue]\n- field: order_date; widget type: date-range; layout: { row: 8, column: 4, width: 4, height: 1 }; applies to widgets: [Total Revenue, Revenue by Region, Daily Revenue]\n```\n\n## Field reference conventions\n\n- **Dimensions**: backtick the field name only when it contains spaces or special characters. Plain identifiers don't need backticks.\n- **Measures**: always write `MEASURE(<name>)`. The sub-agent translates this into the widget's expression form.\n- **Field names** must match the metric view's dimension/measure names exactly — case-sensitive.\n- **`format:` belongs on the quantitative column object** (`y.format`, `value.format`, `yAxisPrimary[].format`, `target.format`), **not under `options:`**. The widget schema's `NumberFormatType` is a property of `QuantitativeColumnType`, not of the widget. The keys are `formatType` (one of `number` / `percent` / `currency`), `decimalPlaces`, `abbreviation` (`none` or `compact`), and `currencyCode` — there is no top-level `type` key.\n\n## Layout constraints\n\n- 12-column grid: `column + width ≤ 12`, `width ≥ 1`, `height ≥ 1`.\n- Counter / KPI widgets: `height ≤ 2` (a single number doesn't need to be tall).\n- Filter widgets: typically `height = 1`.\n\n## What NOT to include\n\n- Don't reuse a single per-page prompt across pages by find-and-replace; each prompt must be reviewed for that page's specific filters, sort, and layout.\n- Don't include the source `Report/Layout` JSON in the prompt; the sub-agent doesn't need it and it bloats the call.\n- Don't omit the \"applies to widgets\" wiring on a page filter — without it, the filter widget appears but does nothing.\n- Don't carry over Power BI report-level filters into the prompt by default; the agent typically lifts them to dashboard-level filters via a separate global filter widget shared across pages.\n"}}]);
